#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Drive an Ur/Web application in a browser, step by step, on one logical
clock, and record what the pages show after each step; Chromium, Firefox
or WebKit, through Playwright for Python.

    urt-browse [--engine chromium|firefox|webkit] [--epoch SECONDS]
               [--timeout SECONDS] [--screenshots DIR] [--recordings DIR]
               URL RUN OUT
    urt-browse --serve [the same options but the directories]
    urt-browse --check RUN

URL is where the application listens (http://127.0.0.1:8000), its clock
pinned (URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_EPOCH, and URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_TASKS=last); RUN
a run's directory: the NAME.step files under RUN/interaction, done in the
order of their names, and RUN/context, how the users' browsers are set
up (below); OUT where the records go: after every step, one set per tab
that is open, USER.TAB being the user's name and the tab's, and one for
the step:

    NAME.USER.TAB.page.html
                    the page, as HTML: its path in a comment, then the DOM
                    as the browser has it, one element per line, without
                    scripts, styles and handlers, with the live value of
                    every field; `hidden` on what is not shown, and
                    `autofocus` on the field that has the focus when that
                    is one typed into (an input of a text kind, a
                    textarea, an editable element): the engines agree on
                    those, where whether a button or a checkbox takes the
                    focus on a click is the platform's convention
    NAME.USER.TAB.requests
                    what the page fetched, in the order it asked, with the
                    status that came back, or 'aborted' when the page left
                    it behind; a request still open when the step is over
                    (the /.msgs poll) is marked '...', and is listed again,
                    with its status, after the own requests of the step in
                    which it came back
    NAME.USER.TAB.dialogs
                    the alerts, confirms and prompts, with the answer given
    NAME.USER.TAB.console
                    what the page logged, and the errors its script threw
    NAME.timeline   what happened when, on the clock: every line of the
                    step, from when the user took it to when it was done,
                    and every request, from when the page sent it to when
                    the answer came back; seconds since the epoch
    NAME.error      why the step could not be done; the run stops there

the dialogs, the console and the error only if there was anything.
With --screenshots, every tab is also shot after every step, the whole
page as the engine draws it, into DIR as NAME.USER.TAB.ENGINE.png: for
looking at, not for comparing.  With --recordings, every tab is filmed
from its opening to its closing, into DIR as USER.TAB.ENGINE.webm, with
a pointer drawn in and the lines as cues (USER.TAB.ENGINE.vtt), and an
index.html in DIR plays them.  The records are the same with and without
either.  After each step a line 'step NAME' is printed; a step that
cannot be done prints 'fail NAME' and ends the run; 'done' ends it
otherwise.  With --serve, the browser is started once and a run is done
for every line 'run URL RUN OUT [screenshots=DIR] [recordings=DIR]' read
from stdin, quoted as for a shell.  With --check, the step and context
files are read and nothing is run: what is wrong with them is printed,
a line each, and the exit status is 1 if anything was.

The clock.  There is one, for the pages (Playwright's clock, moved by
the driver), the server (told the time of every request, and moved
through GET /.clock) and the users.  It starts at the epoch and moves
from one thing due to the next, as fast as they get done, nothing
waiting on the wall clock: a user's act takes the time it takes a person
(below), a request reaches the server half a round trip after the page
sent it and its answer the page half a round trip later, a page's timer
fires when it is due, a task of the server's when it is due.  What is
due at the same time is done in a fixed order: the server's part first,
its tasks and the requests arriving, in the order the context's tie rule
says; then the pages' timers and the answers coming back; then the
users' input, users in the tie order.  A run comes out the same every
time, whatever the machine.

What moves on its own in a page is on the clock too.  An animation (a
CSS transition or animation, element.animate()) is held where the clock
says, and its start, iterations and end, with their events, come at
their time; an endless one's iterations do not, and it never ends.  The
engines' scrolling is instant, whatever the page's CSS says, and a
smooth scroll the page asks for takes 300ms, a frame every 16ms.  The
tab in front of a user is visible and has the focus, the others are
hidden and get no animation frames: visibilitychange, blur and focus
come when the front changes, and a dialog a hidden tab shows waits for
the user to come to it.  Code running on after a dialog it showed sees
the time at which it showed until its next timer or event.

A step file holds lines, each for a user: 'USER: ACTION', or a line
'USER:' alone and under it the user's lines, each indented with one
tab.  A user is a browser of their own, cookies and all; their tabs are
pages in it, each a client to the application, and their lines act on
the tab in front.  The users of a step go side by side from its start,
each through their lines in order; the step is over when all are done
and no page has a request open but its poll, and then it is recorded.
A last line 'wait T', for no user, lets T pass for everyone before the
records are taken.  Blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped.

In a line, text and locators are in single quotes, as for a shell
('it'\\''s'); double quotes are for labels.  After an action's
arguments come its parameters, in any order: [T], its time, a number
and its unit (500ms, 2s, 1.5s, 3min, 1h, 2d); [NAME=VALUE], one it may
be given; (NAME=VALUE), one it must; [NAME], a flag.  L is a locator in
Playwright's syntax ('text=Batch it', 'button >> nth=1', '#uw3'); it
must match one element, and an action waits until it does, shown,
enabled and still, at most as long as an until.  Still is no animation
of its own or around it under way and, while anything else in the page
moves, the same box on two frames in a row, 16ms apart.  X,Y is a
position on an element, each a percentage of its box or pixels from its
top-left corner.

    open PATH [T]           go to the page at PATH, starting with /
    click L [at=X,Y] [T]    also dblclick, hover: the pointer moves to the
                            element, a move a frame, then presses and lets go
    focus L [T]             also check, uncheck
    drag L1 L2 [from=X,Y] [to=X,Y] [T]
                            press on L1, move to L2, come to rest, let go
    swipe L (from=X,Y) (to=X,Y) [T]
                            press, move within L, let go while moving
    wheel DX DY [T]         the mouse wheel where the pointer is, DX right
                            and DY down, in pixels: the page gets the
                            event, and unless it cancels it, the nearest
                            box that can scroll that way moves that much,
                            at once
    fill L TEXT [T]         set a field to TEXT
    type L TEXT [T]         type TEXT into it, a key at a time; T a key
    press KEY [on=L] [T]    Enter, Tab, ArrowDown, Control+a, ...
    select L VALUE... [T]   choose in a select
    back [T]                also forward, reload
    close [T]               close the tab in front; the tab after it in the
                            order of opening comes to the front, or the one
                            before it if it was the last
    tab NAME [T]            bring the tab NAME to the front
    quit                    close the user's browser, every tab, every cookie
    dialog accept [T]       the answer to the next dialog of the tab in
                            front, T after it shows; also 'dialog dismiss'
                            and 'dialog answer TEXT'; unanswered, accepted
    until L [gone] [T]      until L is shown (gone: is not), at most T
    wait T                  let T pass
    label "NAME"            a point in the user's lines
    after USER."NAME"       until that user has passed that label

The page actions (open, click, ..., close) take [target=NAME], naming
the tab the action opens: open with it opens a new tab, a click a tab
the page opens; a tab comes only with a name, and is called by it.  A
user starts with no tab.  An action ends when what it set off is done --
its requests answered, and those sent on their answers, the pages it
loads loaded, its dialogs answered -- not timers due later, nor the poll;
with [async] the user goes on once their own act is done.  The user's
browser lasts until the run ends or they quit, also with no tab open:
if closing the last tab closed it, whether a close lost the user's
cookies would depend on whether another of their tabs happened to be
open, and a user who leaves the site and comes back could not be written.

The context file, OPTION VALUE a line, sets up every user with what is
before the first line [USER], and that user with what is under it:

    order Alice Bernd       the users in the order what they do at the same
                            time is done; a run with more than one user has it
    tasks first|last        the server's tasks due when requests arrive come
                            before them (the default) or after
    rtt T                   a request's round trip, 100ms unless given
    throttle AFTER EVERY [chained]
                            once a tab has been hidden for AFTER, a timer of
                            its page due meanwhile waits for the next multiple
                            of EVERY on the clock, with chained only one set
                            from a timer five deep; a line a tier, the last
                            that applies counting, and the tab's return
                            running what waits.  Unless given, Chrome's:
                            'throttle 0s 1s' and 'throttle 5min 1min
                            chained'; 'throttle none' for no throttling.  A
                            user's lines take the place of everyone's
    VERB T                  how long the verb's act takes unless an action
                            says: click 400ms, dblclick, hover, check, uncheck
                            400ms, drag 1s, swipe 300ms, wheel 300ms, type
                            150ms a key, fill 200ms, press 100ms, select
                            500ms, focus 100ms, tab 300ms, dialog 1s, until
                            10s at most; open, back, forward, reload, close 0
    viewport WxH            the window; 1024x768 unless given
    device_scale_factor N   1
    color_scheme light|dark|no-preference
    reduced_motion reduce|no-preference
    forced_colors active|none
    locale TAG              en-US
    timezone_id ZONE        UTC
    has_touch yes|no
    user_agent STRING       urt

order and tasks are the run's alone; what is not given is the engine's
own.  Every request reaches the server: no engine caches, none fetches a
favicon.  A request for any other server is refused, and recorded as
such: a run drives this application, and the network is not its
business.
"""

